From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mcla-x86 SDK patch layer
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 04e0: tonemap_transfer_skip: skip the full-screen ownership transfer that binding the tone map's 8888 target causes (the tone map rewrites every pixel); poison check, [tonemap-skip] log

Source: Ours, written for this SDK (O4; R2 finding 1 and R4 change 3 of $WORK/scratch/R2/REPORT.md and R4/REPORT.md, P1 for the
  cost). Builds on 0450's analysis (RenderTargetCache::GetOverwrittenRectangle, Renan Queiroz's rexglue-skip-overwritten-
  transfers idea, re-done in 0450). Not run in the game: clang++ 20.1.8 -fsyntax-only (the SDK's Release flags) of the four
  changed translation units, nothing more
Licence: GPL-3 (owner's own work, part of mcla-x86); the patched files are ReXGlue SDK code (BSD-3-Clause)
Purpose: At 1080p single tile (mcla_ws_single_tile) the scene colour (7e3, host R16G16B16A16F, 1920x1088) and the game's tone
  map / HUD target (8_8_8_8, 1920x1080) are the same EDRAM tiles 1632..3263 at pitch 24. Binding the 8888 target for the tone
  map takes the range over from the scene colour, so ChangeOwnership queues a transfer of all 1632 tiles (2.09 Mpx RGBA16F ->
  RGBA8, about 16.7 MB read and 8.4 MB written, a raster pass and its barriers: 0.55-0.6 ms of the P1 frame when it is
  present), and the tone map then overwrites every pixel of it. skip_overwritten_transfers (0450) does that for every
  rectangle draw and is not usable (S1: +6 ms GPU). This patch does it for the tone map only.
  New cvars (both hot reload): tonemap_transfer_skip (bool, default false) and tonemap_transfer_skip_poison (bool, default
  false). When tonemap_transfer_skip is on and skip_overwritten_transfers is off (the generic one takes precedence and
  behaves as before), RenderTargetCache::Update does the following for a draw, and for no other:
    1. the title's tone map marker (GuestMarkerKind::kTonemapBegin, written by the game hook mcla/hooks/mcla_fsr_markers.cpp
       at the start of renderer phase 0x10000000) has been seen in this frame: CommandProcessor::hdr_frame_.tonemap, which
       OnGuestMarker sets and the swap clears. The backends' IssueDraw (D3D12 and Vulkan) hand it over with the new
       RenderTargetCache::SetUpdateAfterTonemapMarker(), like SetUpdatePixelShader, for every draw (Update forgets it);
    2. the draw is a rectangle list (VGT_DRAW_INITIATOR) and its ownership change would need transfers
       (WouldOwnershipChangeRequireTransfers), i.e. it is the draw that binds the target (a tone map draw that finds the
       range already owned has nothing to skip);
    3. its pixel shader is E5EBEFAE4D165CBA (the tone map's; the HDR scene check in command_processor.cpp keys the same
       shader; the vertex shader, 1FAB9787BCA32D7C, is not keyed: the analysis runs whatever it is on the CPU);
    4. GetOverwrittenRectangle proves that it rewrites a rectangle of its colour targets completely (3 auto-indexed vertices
       that are corners of one axis-aligned rectangle, no user clip planes, no depth or stencil, every component of every
       written colour target replaced, blend One/Zero/Add, no kill / alpha test / alpha to mask, not clipped by the near or
       far plane, the vertices not depending on fetches, MSAA up to 4x - 0450's analysis, unchanged), AND the new optional
       output whole_out says that the rectangle starts at 0,0 and reaches the far side of everything it is cut to (viewport,
       scissor, render target): the whole screen. A rectangle that proves less (e.g. a 1 pixel short edge) is kept (the
       transfer is made as before), whatever the analysis says, and said so in the log.
  Then the rectangle goes to ChangeOwnership as the cutout, exactly as 0450's does: nothing is copied into it; what the
  rectangle does not cover (the 8 rows of the 1088 high host target below the 1080 row scissor) is transferred as before, so
  the picture is exact by the same argument as 0450's (the cut region is overwritten by the draw before anything reads it).
  tonemap_transfer_skip_poison: after the ownership change, the cut rectangle is cleared to magenta (host value 1,0,1,1, the
  colour render targets of the draw) before the draw, so a pixel the tone map does not write shows in the picture; a picture
  with the skip and the poison on must equal one with both off.
  Log (every 10 s while tonemap_transfer_skip acts, and once at the first skip and once per reason a transfer was kept):
    [tonemap-skip] N transfers skipped, M kept (<reason> xK; ...) in 10.0 s; the tone map marker was seen in F frames, O other
    rectangle draws after it needed transfers (the first: pixel shader ...)
  N + M is the number of tone map draws that needed the transfer; F zero says the markers are off ("- no marker: ..."); O > 0
  with N + M = 0 means the first draw that binds the target is not the tone map (the shader is named).
  The markers: the game hook writes them only while mcla_render_markers is 1, or -1 (auto) with temporal_aa=fsr; it now also
  does with tonemap_transfer_skip on (mcla/hooks/mcla_fsr_markers.cpp, game side).
  Cost with the cvar off: one extra bool store in Update and a cvar read; GetOverwrittenRectangle's other callers are
  unchanged (whole_out defaults to null). The Vulkan backend gets the same call site, not exercised.
Bucket: REDO

04e0-tonemap-transfer-skip.patch (mcla-x86 O4; $WORK/scratch/O4)

Check (frozen world, per 0450's protocol: free roam, pause menu, driving; 1080p single tile preset): --tonemap_transfer_skip=
true --tonemap_transfer_skip_poison=true against both off must show no differing pixel (no magenta anywhere), and the log must
say N > 0 transfers skipped, 0 kept; GPU timing with the poison off. If it says kept (<reason>), the reason names what the
analysis could not prove.

